      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > April 24, 2026 release",
      "text": "**Why**:\n\n- These updates were guided directly by user testing, which highlighted the need for clearer structure, more intuitive navigation, and more explanatory text. Improving readability, comprehension, and alignment with plain language best practices were central priorities throughout the revision process. By refining the layout, adding supportive visuals, and clarifying key concepts, the updated page aims to help visitors of all backgrounds more easily understand the burden of respiratory illness and interpret their potential risk. The reorganization and enhanced guidance are intended to provide a more accessible, informative, and user-friendly experience across the Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel.\n\n3\\. Revision of \"Hospitalization Rates: NHSN\" Data Notes\n\nData Notes for \"Hospitalization Rates: NHSN\" have been updated to explain how CDC determines the level of hospital admissions shown in the page's newest graphic.\n\nAdditional revisions were made to clarify calculations related to other NHSN metrics, like hospitalization rate."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#41",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > April 10, 2026 release",
      "text": "1. NWSS Data Pipeline and Visual Update\n\n- CDC's National Wastewater Surveillance System updated their data pipelines to refine their wastewater viral activity level thresholds and transitioned their map's color scheme to align with PRISM's alert colors."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#37",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > August 14, 2026 release",
      "text": "**Why:**\n\n- These updates improve audience understanding of the Respiratory Illness Data Channel by putting foundational epidemiological concepts into plain language. By defining how data sources fit together, as well as identifying unknowns, individuals can better understand their disease risk in their community."
