      "heading": "ObsAwareData methods > 5. Known limitations",
      "text": "1. **The flag thresholds are provisional.** They are data-derived and have not been externally or clinically validated. Treat the flags as screening aids, not ground truth.\n2. **Coverage flags are static for ED and wastewater.** The NSSP participation status reflects a single August 2026 facility map. The NWSS participation status is one summary per site and pathogen in each source release. Neither describes how coverage changed over time.\n3. **Some region IDs repeat across region types.** `analysis_region_id` is unique only together with `analysis_region_type`. In Pennsylvania, the HSA with code `42` and the state FIPS code `42` coexist.\n4. **Source coverage varies by state and period.**\n   - NSSP data begin in October 2022.\n   - The NSSP `combined` series ends in September 2024 in most states.\n   - NHSN influenza reporting in most states begins in October 2020.\n   - North Dakota has no influenza A or RSV wastewater data. Its COVID-19 wastewater data (2 sites) end on 2023-09-16."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "mmwr_ww_ed_wi#14",
      "doc_id": "mmwr_ww_ed_wi",
      "heading": "MMWR: Wastewater as a complement to ED visit data, influenza A and RSV, Wisconsin 2022\u201323 > Discussion > Limitations",
      "text": "The findings in this report are subject to at least four limitations. First, wastewater surveillance does not reflect disease patterns among residents not served by municipal wastewater infrastructure and thus would not be representative of households using septic systems. Similarly, wastewater surveillance would likely fail to collect data from diapered children, that might be important in surveillance for RSV, which predominates among young children. Second, surveillance data in both systems were based on weekly numbers, which might obscure nuanced outbreak or cluster patterns in a community. Third, the seasonal outbreak dynamics of influenza and RSV vary from year to year, and because this analysis only includes a small data set from one respiratory season, results presented here might not reflect disease patterns in all years. Finally, variability in wastewater concentrations for each community, and thus differences in temporal patterns between wastewater and ED data, could be attributable to a number of factors independent of the incidence in each community (e.g., wastewater temperature and pH and the presence of external chemical inhibitors) (*10*)."
